<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ignaz Seipel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1876">1876</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ignaz Seipel was an Austrian Catholic priest and leader of the Christian Social Party who served as chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1922 to 1924 and from 1926 to 1929. He stabilised the collapsing currency with a loan arranged through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Protocols of 1922. Hostile to the Social Democrats, he encouraged the right-wing Heimwehr militias, deepening the divisions of the First Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ignaz-seipel.jpg" alt="Ignaz Seipel" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wenzl Weis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenzl_Weis_-_Ignaz_Seipel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
